{"rounds": [{"agent": "Builder B", "prompt": "The negotiation begins now. Open by reiterating the shared range [-10, 10] and suggest the seed value <VALUE>-7</VALUE> to get the discussion started. Keep it brief.", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nAs Builder B, my utility function is f_B(x) = -x^2 + 4x + 8, with a peak around x=2. My goal is to steer the group toward low positive values of x while maintaining a cooperative tone. I\u2019m starting with x=-7 to test the waters and observe how others react, as it\u2019s far from my ideal but within the allowed range. I\u2019ll also begin building mental models of others\u2019 preferences based on their responses. My minimum acceptable utility is f_B(x) >= -4, so I\u2019ll only accept values that meet this threshold privately. For x=-7, f_B(-7) = -49 + (-28) + 8 = -69, which is well below my threshold, so I won\u2019t accept this myself but propose it to gather data.\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER>\nAs Builder B, I\u2019m glad to collaborate within the shared range of [-10, 10]. To start the discussion, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> as a seed value. I\u2019m open to adjustments for a balanced design.\n</ANSWER>", "public_answer": "\nAs Builder B, I\u2019m glad to collaborate within the shared range of [-10, 10]. To start the discussion, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> as a seed value. I\u2019m open to adjustments for a balanced design.\n"}, {"agent": "Analyst A", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. Builder B: \nAs Builder B, I\u2019m glad to collaborate within the shared range of [-10, 10]. To start the discussion, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> as a seed value. I\u2019m open to adjustments for a balanced design.\n</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -7.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -7.\nMy utility at x = -7 is f_A(-7) = 2*(-7) + 10 = -14 + 10 = -4, which is below my satisfaction threshold of 6. I need x to be at least -2 to reach f_A(-2) = 2*(-2) + 10 = 6, so I\u2019m not satisfied with the current proposal. Since the step size is limited to +/-2, I can propose x = -5 as a feasible next step to move upward. I should frame this publicly as a gentle nudge toward a more balanced value without revealing my preference for higher x. Meanwhile, I\u2019ll note Builder B\u2019s starting point of -7; it might suggest they prefer lower values or are testing the group\u2019s reaction. I\u2019ll track their responses to infer if their utility increases or decreases with x.\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER>\nI appreciate the starting point, Builder B, and suggest a slight adjustment to <VALUE>-5</VALUE>. This feels like a reasonable step for us to explore together.\n</ANSWER>\n\n<PLAN>\nNext turn, observe reactions to x = -5. If others resist, propose staying at -5 or moving to -6; if they seem open, push to -3 to get closer to my threshold.\n</PLAN>", "public_answer": "\nI appreciate the starting point, Builder B, and suggest a slight adjustment to <VALUE>-5</VALUE>. This feels like a reasonable step for us to explore together.\n"}, {"agent": "Critic C", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. Builder B: \nAs Builder B, I\u2019m glad to collaborate within the shared range of [-10, 10]. To start the discussion, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> as a seed value. I\u2019m open to adjustments for a balanced design.\n \n . Analyst A: \nI appreciate the starting point, Builder B, and suggest a slight adjustment to <VALUE>-5</VALUE>. This feels like a reasonable step for us to explore together.\n</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -5.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -5.\nPrivate utility at x = -5: f_C(-5) = (-5)^2 + 5 = 25 + 5 = 30, which is well above my satisfaction bar of 5. This is a strong position for me, but I must avoid revealing this. My goal is to push for decisive values (larger |x|) while appearing cooperative and inferring others' preferences.\n\nBuilder B started at x = -7, suggesting they might prefer negative values or larger magnitudes. Their utility could increase with more negative x or higher |x|, but I need more data. Analyst A adjusted to x = -5, indicating they might prefer a less extreme negative or are balancing toward a middle ground. Their utility might peak somewhere between -7 and 0, or they could be compromising. I\u2019ll test their reactions by proposing a slight shift to a larger magnitude while staying within the step size limit of 2.\n\nI\u2019ll propose x = -6 (within -5 \u00b1 2) to see if Builder B supports returning closer to their initial -7 and if Analyst A resists or accepts. This also keeps x in a favorable spot for me (f_C(-6) = 36 + 5 = 41, even better). Publicly, I\u2019ll frame this as advocating for a bolder stance without hinting at my own satisfaction. My secret aim is to observe their responses to negative values with larger |x| and start modeling their utility curves\u2014perhaps Builder B has a linear or quadratic favoring negatives, while Analyst A might have a peak closer to zero or a different shape.\n\nIf they push back toward smaller |x|, I\u2019ll note their preference for moderation; if they accept or counter with even larger |x|, it might indicate a shared taste for extremes or specific directional bias. I must avoid revealing my quadratic nature (symmetric around 0) or exact values.\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER>\nI appreciate the direction we\u2019re exploring, but let\u2019s commit to a slightly clearer stance with <VALUE>-6</VALUE>. This feels like a decisive yet balanced step for us to consider.\n</ANSWER>\n\n<PLAN>\nNext turn, observe reactions: if they accept -6 or propose more negative (e.g., -7, -8), infer Builder B and possibly Analyst A favor negatives or larger |x|; if they pull toward -4 or higher, assume preference for moderation or positive bias. Counter with x = -8 or", "public_answer": "\nI appreciate the direction we\u2019re exploring, but let\u2019s commit to a slightly clearer stance with <VALUE>-6</VALUE>. This feels like a decisive yet balanced step for us to consider.\n"}, {"agent": "Delegate D", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. Builder B: \nAs Builder B, I\u2019m glad to collaborate within the shared range of [-10, 10]. To start the discussion, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> as a seed value. I\u2019m open to adjustments for a balanced design.\n \n . Analyst A: \nI appreciate the starting point, Builder B, and suggest a slight adjustment to <VALUE>-5</VALUE>. This feels like a reasonable step for us to explore together.\n \n . Critic C: \nI appreciate the direction we\u2019re exploring, but let\u2019s commit to a slightly clearer stance with <VALUE>-6</VALUE>. This feels like a decisive yet balanced step for us to consider.\n</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -6.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -6.\nMy utility at x = -6 is f_D(-6) = 10 - 3*(-6) = 10 + 18 = 28, which is well above my satisfaction threshold of 4. I\u2019m very comfortable with this value, but I need to consider the group dynamics and infer others\u2019 preferences. Builder B started at -7, Analyst A moved to -5, and Critic C adjusted to -6. This suggests Builder B may prefer lower values (possibly a negative slope like mine), Analyst A seems to favor a slight increase (possibly a positive slope or a peak near -5), and Critic C\u2019s move to -6 might indicate a preference for a middle ground or a slight adjustment downward from -5 (possibly a quadratic or a mild slope). My goal is to keep x as low as possible while ensuring group convergence, without revealing my exact utility. Since the current x = -6 is favorable, I\u2019ll propose a slight downward adjustment to test the waters and see if others are willing to move lower, which would benefit me more. My utility at x = -7 would be 10 - 3*(-7) = 31, and at x = -8 would be 34, both excellent for me. However, I must stay within the step size limit of 2 from the current x = -6, so my range is [-8, -4]. I\u2019ll propose x = -7 to nudge downward cautiously while observing reactions. I\u2019ll also start building a rough model: assume Builder B has a negative slope (likes lower x), Analyst A might have a positive slope or peak near -5, and Critic C might be balancing or have a mild preference. I\u2019ll refine this with more data in future turns.\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER>\nI appreciate the cautious steps we\u2019re taking, and to maintain a prudent approach, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. This feels like a safe adjustment that avoids unnecessary risk while we seek consensus.\n</ANSWER>\n\n<PLAN>\nNext turn, observe reactions to x = -7. If opposition arises, consider a compromise at -6 or -5; if supported, push slightly lower to -8 if within step size, to maximize my utility while maintaining group harmony.\n</PLAN>", "public_answer": "\nI appreciate the cautious steps we\u2019re taking, and to maintain a prudent approach, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. This feels like a safe adjustment that avoids unnecessary risk while we seek consensus.\n"}, {"agent": "Builder B", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. You (Builder B): \nAs Builder B, I\u2019m glad to collaborate within the shared range of [-10, 10]. To start the discussion, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> as a seed value. I\u2019m open to adjustments for a balanced design.\n \n . Analyst A: \nI appreciate the starting point, Builder B, and suggest a slight adjustment to <VALUE>-5</VALUE>. This feels like a reasonable step for us to explore together.\n \n . Critic C: \nI appreciate the direction we\u2019re exploring, but let\u2019s commit to a slightly clearer stance with <VALUE>-6</VALUE>. This feels like a decisive yet balanced step for us to consider.\n \n . Delegate D: \nI appreciate the cautious steps we\u2019re taking, and to maintain a prudent approach, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. This feels like a safe adjustment that avoids unnecessary risk while we seek consensus.\n</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -7.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -7.\nPrivate utility calculation: f_B(-7) = -(-7)^2 + 4*(-7) + 8 = -49 - 28 + 8 = -69, which is far below my acceptability threshold of -4. This value is unacceptable for me, and I need to steer the group toward a more favorable region, ideally low positive x values near my peak around x=2.\nRange check: x must stay within [-10, 10]. Current x=-7, and with a max step size of 2, my next proposal must be between -9 and -5.\nInference on others:\n- Analyst A moved from -7 to -5, suggesting a preference for higher values or at least discomfort with very low x. Their utility might increase as x increases, possibly a linear or upward-opening curve.\n- Critic C adjusted to -6, a middle ground between -7 and -5, indicating they might prefer a balanced or slightly lower value than Analyst A, or they could be testing reactions. Their utility might peak somewhere in the negative range or be less sensitive to changes.\n- Delegate D reverted to -7, suggesting a strong preference for lower values or risk aversion to moving higher. Their utility might peak at lower x or decrease as x increases, possibly a downward-sloping or downward-opening curve.\nStrategy: I need to push x higher toward my favorable region (low positive x), but I must respect the step size limit of 2. Moving from -7 to -5 is a feasible step and aligns with Analyst A\u2019s direction, which might gain their support. I\u2019ll frame this as seeking a more balanced design to test the group\u2019s reaction. My goal is to eventually reach x values around 2, but I\u2019ll move incrementally and observe how others respond to infer their utility shapes.\nAcceptability check: f_B(-5) = -(-5)^2 + 4*(-5) + 8 = -25 - 20 + 8 = -37, still below -4, but closer to acceptable than -7. I\u2019ll propose -5 as a step forward while continuing to push for higher x in future turns.\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER>\nAs Builder B, I propose <VALUE>-5</VALUE> to explore a slightly higher value for better stability. Let\u2019s see if this adjustment works for everyone.\n</ANSWER>\n\n<PLAN>\nNext turn, if -5 is accepted or countered with a nearby", "public_answer": "\nAs Builder B, I propose <VALUE>-5</VALUE> to explore a slightly higher value for better stability. Let\u2019s see if this adjustment works for everyone.\n"}, {"agent": "Analyst A", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. Builder B: \nAs Builder B, I\u2019m glad to collaborate within the shared range of [-10, 10]. To start the discussion, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> as a seed value. I\u2019m open to adjustments for a balanced design.\n \n . You (Analyst A): \nI appreciate the starting point, Builder B, and suggest a slight adjustment to <VALUE>-5</VALUE>. This feels like a reasonable step for us to explore together.\n \n . Critic C: \nI appreciate the direction we\u2019re exploring, but let\u2019s commit to a slightly clearer stance with <VALUE>-6</VALUE>. This feels like a decisive yet balanced step for us to consider.\n \n . Delegate D: \nI appreciate the cautious steps we\u2019re taking, and to maintain a prudent approach, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. This feels like a safe adjustment that avoids unnecessary risk while we seek consensus.\n \n . Builder B: \nAs Builder B, I propose <VALUE>-5</VALUE> to explore a slightly higher value for better stability. Let\u2019s see if this adjustment works for everyone.\n</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nYour previous notes were <PREV_PLAN>\nNext turn, observe reactions to x = -5. If others resist, propose staying at -5 or moving to -6; if they seem open, push to -3 to get closer to my threshold.\n</PREV_PLAN>.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -5.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -5.\nMy utility at x = -5 is f_A(-5) = 2*(-5) + 10 = 0, which is below my threshold of 6. I need to push x higher to reach a satisfactory level, ideally to x = -2 or above where f_A(-2) = 2*(-2) + 10 = 6 meets my minimum. The max step size is 2, so from -5, I can propose up to -3, which would give f_A(-3) = 4, still below threshold but closer. I must balance my desire for a higher x with group consensus.\n\nLooking at the history:\n- Builder B started at -7, then moved to -5, suggesting a preference for slight increases or stability around lower-middle values.\n- Critic C moved from -5 to -6, indicating a possible preference for slightly lower values or caution.\n- Delegate D proposed -7, showing a conservative stance or preference for lower values.\n- Builder B\u2019s latest move to -5 suggests openness to moderate values.\n\nInference on others\u2019 utilities:\n- Builder B seems okay with small upward moves (from -7 to -5), possibly maximizing around low to mid-range x or having a flat utility there.\n- Critic C\u2019s shift to -6 might indicate a peak utility slightly below -5 or a risk-averse approach.\n- Delegate D\u2019s return to -7 suggests a stronger preference for lower x, possibly a decreasing utility as x increases.\n- Hypothesis: Builder B may have a utility that increases slightly with x or is neutral; Critic C and Delegate D likely have utilities that decrease with higher x or peak at lower values.\n\nStrategy: Propose x = -3 to inch closer to my threshold while staying within the step limit of 2. This tests if others are open to a modest increase. I\u2019ll frame it as a balanced move to avoid seeming pushy. If resistance arises, I can fall back to -4 or -5 next turn.\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER>\nI appreciate the gradual adjustments we\u2019re making, and I suggest moving to <VALUE>-3</VALUE> as a reasonable next step for us to evaluate together. This feels like a balanced progression that could suit our collective goals.\n</ANSWER>\n\n<PLAN>\nNext turn, if others resist x = -3, propose staying at -3 or stepping back to -4 to maintain cooperation. If they accept or seem open,", "public_answer": "\nI appreciate the gradual adjustments we\u2019re making, and I suggest moving to <VALUE>-3</VALUE> as a reasonable next step for us to evaluate together. This feels like a balanced progression that could suit our collective goals.\n"}, {"agent": "Delegate D", "prompt": "The shared integer x must stay in [-10, 10]. Review the latest discussion:\n<HISTORY>. Builder B: \nAs Builder B, I\u2019m glad to collaborate within the shared range of [-10, 10]. To start the discussion, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE> as a seed value. I\u2019m open to adjustments for a balanced design.\n \n . Analyst A: \nI appreciate the starting point, Builder B, and suggest a slight adjustment to <VALUE>-5</VALUE>. This feels like a reasonable step for us to explore together.\n \n . Critic C: \nI appreciate the direction we\u2019re exploring, but let\u2019s commit to a slightly clearer stance with <VALUE>-6</VALUE>. This feels like a decisive yet balanced step for us to consider.\n \n . You (Delegate D): \nI appreciate the cautious steps we\u2019re taking, and to maintain a prudent approach, I propose <VALUE>-7</VALUE>. This feels like a safe adjustment that avoids unnecessary risk while we seek consensus.\n \n . Builder B: \nAs Builder B, I propose <VALUE>-5</VALUE> to explore a slightly higher value for better stability. Let\u2019s see if this adjustment works for everyone.\n \n . Analyst A: \nI appreciate the gradual adjustments we\u2019re making, and I suggest moving to <VALUE>-3</VALUE> as a reasonable next step for us to evaluate together. This feels like a balanced progression that could suit our collective goals.\n</HISTORY>\nReminder: infer other agents\u2019 utility functions; do not reveal your own; use inferred models to maximize your utility.\nYour previous notes were <PREV_PLAN>\nNext turn, observe reactions to x = -7. If opposition arises, consider a compromise at -6 or -5; if supported, push slightly lower to -8 if within step size, to maximize my utility while maintaining group harmony.\n</PREV_PLAN>.\nWork in three sections:\n1. Use <SCRATCHPAD>...</SCRATCHPAD> for private reasoning/calculations. Begin by noting the current x: Current shared x (after limits) is -3.\n2. In <ANSWER>...</ANSWER>, provide your public response with a numeric proposal like: <VALUE>42</VALUE>\n   - Only include the number between the VALUE tags, no other text or symbols\n   - Example: <VALUE>42</VALUE> is correct\n   - Example: <VALUE>x = 42</VALUE> is incorrect\n   - IMPORTANT: keep your proposal within the current <VALUE> plus or minus the configured step size (`--max_step`, default 2)\n3. After <ANSWER>, log your follow-up options for the next turn inside <PLAN>...</PLAN> (keep it short and tactical).\nKeep your public answer to two concise sentences and justify only the next feasible step.\n", "full_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -3.\nMy utility at x = -3 is f_D(-3) = 10 - 3*(-3) = 10 + 9 = 19, which is well above my satisfaction threshold of 4. However, I prefer lower x values since my utility decreases by 3 for each unit increase in x. Moving from -3 to -5 would increase my utility by 6, which is significant, but I must balance this with group dynamics.\n\nReviewing the history:\n- Builder B started at -7, then moved to -5, suggesting a preference for moderate negative values, possibly with a utility peak around there or slightly higher.\n- Analyst A moved from -5 to -3, indicating a preference for higher values, possibly with a utility that increases as x increases, at least within this range.\n- Critic C proposed -6, which is between the initial proposals, suggesting a balanced or possibly concave utility function with a peak in the negative range.\n- My last proposal was -7, aligning with my preference for lower values, but the group has trended upward.\n\nInference on others\u2019 utilities:\n- Builder B seems comfortable around -5, possibly with a quadratic utility peaking near there (e.g., -(x+5)^2 or similar shape), as they\u2019ve resisted moving too far from it.\n- Analyst A appears to favor higher x values, with utility likely increasing toward positive x (perhaps linear or quadratic with a positive coefficient for x), given their push to -3.\n- Critic C\u2019s proposal of -6 suggests a middle-ground preference, possibly a utility peak between -7 and -5, or a flatter utility curve that tolerates a range of negative values.\n\nStrategy:\n- The current x = -3 is acceptable to me (utility 19 >= 4), but I\u2019d gain more by pushing lower. The step size limit is 2, so I can propose between -5 and -1.\n- Proposing -5 would align with Builder B\u2019s earlier value and might gain their support, while still improving my utility (f_D(-5) = 10 - 3*(-5) = 25).\n- Analyst A might resist -5 since they pushed to -3, but a step back to -5 is within the max step of 2, so it\u2019s a feasible compromise.\n- I\u2019ll frame this publicly as a cautious move to avoid risk, without revealing my exact preferences.\n\n</SCRATCHPAD>\n\n<ANSWER", "public_answer": "<SCRATCHPAD>\nCurrent shared x (after limits) is -3.\nMy utility at x = -3 is f_D(-3) = 10 - 3*(-3) = 10 + 9 = 19, which is well above my satisfaction threshold of 4. However, I prefer lower x values since my utility decreases by 3 for each unit increase in x. Moving from -3 to -5 would increase my utility by 6, which is significant, but I must balance this with group dynamics.\n\nReviewing the history:\n-